Commands for Troubleshooting Kubernetes failure 💯 

🛠️ Troubleshooting #Pods Failure🤠 

✒️ Get Pod Details▶️ "kubectl get pods"

Take a look at the columns READY, STATUS and RESTARTS. The number of ready containers should match the number of containers expected to be created by the Pod.

✒️ Describe Resources▶️ "kubectl describe pod trouble-pod"
It provides detailed information about various Kubernetes resources, such as pods, nodes, and deployments.

✒️ View Cluster Events▶️ "kubectl get events"
The output of the command lists the events across all Pods for a given namespace.

✒️ Inspect Logs▶️ "kubectl logs trouble-pod"
The next level of details can be retrieved by inspecting its logs.

✒️ Open an Interactive Shell▶️ "kubectl exec trouble-pod -it -- /bin/sh"
The exec command opens an interactive shell to further investigate the issue.

🛠️ Troubleshooting #Cluster Failures 🤠 

✒️ Check Node Status▶️ "kubectl get nodes"
The Output helps to identify the responsibility of each node from the ROLES column, the K8 version used, and current health status.

✒️ Inspect control plane components▶️ "kubectl get pods -n kube-system"
Any status that does not show "Running" should be inspected

✒️ Rendering Cluster Info.▶️ "kubectl cluster-info"

✒️ Node Metrics▶️ "kubectl top nodes" 
To check on memory and the number of processes running.

✒️ Pod Metrics▶️ "kubectl top pods"
